    'Natriuretic peptides are released from ventricular myocardium in response to wall stress. In patients with suspected heart failure, a normal BNP or NT-proBNP (e.g., NT-proBNP below 125 pg/mL in the non-acute setting; below 300 pg/mL in acute presentations) makes heart failure unlikely and has high negative predictive value, while raised levels lead to echocardiography. Levels rise with age, renal impairment, atrial fibrillation and pulmonary embolism, and are lower in obesity; sacubitril raises BNP but not NT-proBNP. Troponin indicates myocardial injury; D-dimer is used for thromboembolism.',
