    'Pituitary apoplexy is acute haemorrhage or infarction of a pituitary tumour, sometimes precipitated by anticoagulation, surgery or dopamine agonists. It causes sudden severe headache, vomiting, visual field and acuity loss (chiasmal compression), ophthalmoplegia (cavernous sinus involvement of III, IV and VI), meningism and reduced consciousness. Acute ACTH deficiency causes life-threatening adrenal crisis, so IV hydrocortisone is given immediately after taking blood for cortisol. MRI confirms it; urgent transsphenoidal decompression is indicated for severe or deteriorating visual or neurological deficits. Long-term hypopituitarism is common.',
