    'Graves orbitopathy results from TSH receptor antibodies and other immune mechanisms activating orbital fibroblasts, causing inflammation, expansion of extraocular muscles and orbital fat: lid retraction, proptosis, diplopia, conjunctival injection and, in severe cases, compressive optic neuropathy. Smoking strongly increases risk and severity, so cessation is essential. Radioactive iodine can worsen active orbitopathy and is avoided or covered with oral prednisolone; antithyroid drugs or thyroidectomy are preferred. Moderate–severe active disease is treated with IV methylprednisolone, with mycophenolate, teprotumumab (IGF-1R antibody) or orbital radiotherapy; selenium helps mild disease.',
